## Abstract
The paper develops ML-based surrogate models for finite element analysis (FEA) to accelerate simulation of dynamic mechanical systems. Multiple ML regression algorithms — including decision trees (DTs) and deep neural networks (DNNs) — are compared for predicting time-varying nodal displacements of a 2D truss structure. Extreme gradient-boosting decision trees (XGBoost) are found to outperform other approaches.

## Keywords
- Surrogate modeling
- Finite element analysis (FEA)
- Machine learning
- Artificial neural networks
- Random forest trees
- Gradient boosting regression trees
- Adaptive boosting trees
- Mechanical system analysis

## Research Problem
- Traditional FEA of dynamic mechanical systems is computationally expensive and time-consuming, especially for complex or multi-run simulations (e.g., structural health monitoring, sensor placement optimization).
- Predicting **time-varying displacement** (a serviceability and damage-indicator quantity) across all nodes of a structure requires repeated FE solves that are impractical at scale.
- Existing ML surrogate work largely addresses static systems; application to **transient/dynamic** truss structures had not been well explored.

## Methodology
- **Problem setup:** 2D truss structure subjected to external excitation loadings; target outputs are nodal displacements at 14 nodes in 2 directions (28 outputs total).
- **Training data:** FE simulations used to generate labeled datasets; models were trained with comparatively small datasets relative to prior work.
- **ML algorithms developed and compared:**
  - Decision Trees (DTs)
  - Random Forest (RF) — bagging ensemble
  - Adaptive Boosting (AdaBoost)
  - Gradient Boosting Regression Trees (GBRT)
  - Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost)
  - Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) with varied hidden layers, units, activation functions, and optimizers
- **Challenges addressed:** Under-fitting and over-fitting due to high-dimensional output space (28 outputs from a single-direction input loading).

## Key Findings
- **XGBoost outperformed** all other ML algorithms in predictive accuracy for time-varying nodal displacements.
- ML surrogate models successfully predicted dynamic response **across the entire 2D truss structure**.
- Acceptable surrogate accuracy was achieved with **smaller training datasets** than previously reported in comparable studies.
- DNNs required careful tuning of architecture and optimization strategy to avoid over/under-fitting given the high output dimensionality.

## Contributions
- Development and validation of ML-based surrogate FEA models specifically for **dynamic (transient) mechanical systems** — a less-explored area compared to static surrogates.
- Systematic benchmarking of multiple ML regression families (bagging, boosting, deep learning) on the same structural problem.
- Demonstration that XGBoost is an effective and efficient surrogate for FEA in dynamic structural contexts.
- Potential to significantly reduce computational cost of repeated FE simulations in design, SHM, and maintenance workflows.

## Limitations
- Study is limited to a **2D truss structure**; generalizability to 3D or more geometrically complex systems is not demonstrated.
- Higher-dimensional mechanical systems may require **much larger training datasets**, limiting scalability.
- Input loading is applied in **one direction only**, which may not capture the full complexity of real-world multi-directional excitations.
- The paper text is truncated, so some experimental details and quantitative results may not be fully captured here.

## Conclusion
The study demonstrates that ML surrogate models — particularly XGBoost — can effectively replace repeated FEA runs for estimating the dynamic (time-varying) displacement response of a 2D truss structure. The approach reduces computational overhead while maintaining acceptable predictive accuracy, even with relatively small training datasets. This establishes a practical pathway toward integrating ML surrogates into structural health monitoring, design optimization, and maintenance planning workflows.
